 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "An interval is a set of real numbers between two ends.\n• Closed [a, b]: a ≤ x ≤ b (both ends included). • Open (a, b): a < x < b (ends left out).\n• Half-open [a, b): a ≤ x < b, and (a, b]: a < x ≤ b.\n• Infinite: [a, +∞) means x ≥ a; (−∞, b) means x < b. The side with ∞ is always open.\nOn the number line, a filled dot means the end is included, an open dot means it is not.\n(This lesson writes intervals with brackets and parentheses; some books write ]a, b[ for an open interval: check with your teacher.)",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "a": -2,
   "b": 5
  },
  "problem": "Write {intervalIneq(a, b, true, false)} as an interval and list the whole numbers in it.",
  "steps": [
   "{a} is included: [ ; {b} is not: ).",
   "Interval: {interval(a, b, true, false)}.",
   "Whole numbers: {join(intsIn(a, b, true, false), ', ')}."
  ],
  "answer": "{interval(a, b, true, false)}",
  "checks": []
 },
